hermes v0.23.0: subagents a restart killed are re-dispatched by the first
turn of their session, never dispatched twice, keep the steers sent to
them, and are told which of their background processes died.

Root cause (som, agent fpv, 2026-11-15 22:00 deploy). A 10-task background
delegate_task was parked `resuming` correctly at the restart
(hermes-subagents-resume-after-restart.patch), but its re-dispatch only ran
from two places: the auto-continue kickoff and the session's idle
notification poller, and both need to claim an idle session
(_notif_claim_turn). Right after the restart the poller delivered an older
subagent's finished result first, which started a turn; the kickoff's
re-dispatch then found the session busy and did nothing, so no turn saw the
"they were re-dispatched, do not dispatch them again" note. The model,
seeing its subagents gone ("No live subagent" on steer), dispatched all 10
again by hand; when the session went idle the poller re-dispatched the 10
parked originals too: 20 children for 10 tasks, the manual ones starting
from scratch. Steers the orchestrator had sent before the restart lived
only in the killed children's memory, so the re-dispatched ones ran without
the user's later decisions.

- Every turn re-dispatches first: tui_gateway/prompt_turn.py
  _prepare_turn_input calls _resume_delegations_for_turn, which re-dispatches
  the session's parked units from within the turn (the turn already holds
  the session, so no idle claim) before the model call, and prepends the
  note naming them to the turn's model input. A user message, a delivered
  result, a wake-up and the auto-continue are all covered the same way.
  The in-turn re-dispatch runs in a copy of the turn's contextvars: the
  re-dispatch binds the session context and then clears it (every var set
  to "", not restored), which would otherwise wipe the turn's session key,
  source, UI session id and runtime cwd for the rest of the turn.
- No duplicates: delegate_task drops tasks whose goal is one of the
  session's subagents that run again or wait to (async_delegation
  resuming_goals: live resumed units plus parked rows), and says which unit
  runs each; a call left with nothing returns status "already_running".
  The note tells the model so, points at action='list' for steering, and
  says how to proceed: stop it first (action='stop') or reword the goal; a
  task with `force: true` (new optional task field) is dispatched anyway.
- Steers survive restarts: every unit row records which subagent runs which
  task (task_json.task_subagents). Every delegate_task(action='steer') to a
  ledger task is also stored on the row that runs the task now
  (task_json.steers, by original task index), carried through every
  re-dispatch (resume meta "steers") and put into the re-dispatched child's
  context as binding instructions. A steer to a subagent id that is no
  longer live (killed by a restart) is routed along the chain of
  re-dispatches (same root) to the task's current child, or stored for the
  re-dispatch while the unit is parked, instead of "No live subagent".
  Steers stored between building a re-dispatch plan and its dispatch are
  forwarded to the new unit (session_auto_continue _forward_late_steers).
  The carried list is capped per task across all attempts (newest 20,
  16000 chars in total).
  Ownership: a top-level conversation (depth 0) finds ledger tasks by its
  session id and session key; a delegated orchestrator child inherits the
  parent's session key, so it only finds rows whose parent session is its
  own session id (no steering or storing into a sibling's task).
- N restarts, one child per task: a resume of a resume keeps the original
  unit as root and counts attempts; a task is only ever run by the latest
  row of its root, and the duplicate drop covers every attempt.
- Visibility: action='list' marks re-dispatched children "resumed from
  deleg_X (attempt N)" with the original delegation id, and lists units
  still parked (with their previous subagent ids and queued steers).
- Dead background processes: at the shutdown stop, each unit records the
  running background processes of each child (command redacted with
  force=True, plus whole-value masking of sshpass -p, mysql -pPW, openssl
  -k/-pass, --password=/--token=, and *KEY/TOKEN/SECRET/PASS*=... vars) on
  its ledger row (task_json.dead_processes); the re-dispatch note lists
  them for the child that owned them, so it can check and restart them.

Synchronous delegations: top-level model delegations always run in the
background (run_agent forces background at depth 0), so only an
orchestrator subagent's own workers join inline; those resume as part of
their parent's re-dispatch (the parent re-runs from its transcript), not on
their own.

Tests: tests/tools/test_subagents_resume_every_turn.py and
test_subagents_resume_steer_restarts.py are shipped in this patch but NOT
run by the package build (hermes' test suite is not part of it); run them
by hand against the patched tree. The CI guard is the container check
hyperhermes/checks/resume (checks.x86_64-linux.resume), which fails
without this patch.

Generated against v0.23.0 with every earlier patch of
hyperhermes/modules/core/package.nix applied first.

